## 5. Detailed Dry Run

**Input:**

```java
nums = [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]
```

Initial values:

```java
i = 0;
j = 1;
```

The first element is already considered unique.

| Step | i | j | nums[i] | nums[j] | Action |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | Duplicate, skip |
| 2 | 0 | 2 | 0 | 1 | Increment i, copy 1 |
| 3 | 1 | 3 | 1 | 1 | Duplicate, skip |
| 4 | 1 | 4 | 1 | 1 | Duplicate, skip |
| 5 | 1 | 5 | 1 | 2 | Increment i, copy 2 |
| 6 | 2 | 6 | 2 | 2 | Duplicate, skip |
| 7 | 2 | 7 | 2 | 3 | Increment i, copy 3 |
| 8 | 3 | 8 | 3 | 3 | Duplicate, skip |
| 9 | 3 | 9 | 3 | 4 | Increment i, copy 4 |

Final array:

```text
[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]
```

Remember: elements beyond the first `k` positions do not matter.

Final values:

```java
i = 4;

k = i + 1;

k = 5;
```

The first five elements are:

```text
[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
```

