## 7. Why Does This Approach Work?

The algorithm works because the array is sorted.

All duplicate occurrences of a value appear next to each other.

Therefore, comparing the current element with the last unique element is sufficient.

The pointer `i` always represents the last position containing a unique value.

Whenever a new value appears:

- Move `i` forward.
- Store that value at the new position.

This ensures that unique elements remain in their original sorted order.

---

## 8. Complexity Analysis

Let `n` be the array length.

| Approach | Time Complexity | Extra Space |
|---|---|---|
| Extra Array | O(n) | O(n) |
| Two Pointers | O(n) | O(1) |

**Why is the optimized solution O(n)?**

- Pointer `j` traverses the array once.
- Pointer `i` moves forward only when a new unique element is found.
- Each iteration performs constant-time operations.

Therefore:

\[
Time = O(n)
\]

\[
Space = O(1)
\]

