## 13. Key Takeaways

- This problem uses the Two Pointers technique.
- The input array must be sorted.
- Pointer `i` tracks the last unique element.
- Pointer `j` searches for the next unique element.
- Duplicate elements are skipped.
- New unique elements are copied into the next available position.
- The optimized solution uses O(n) time and O(1) extra space.

**Most important pattern to remember:**

```java
int i = 0;

for (int j = 1; j < nums.length; j++) {

    if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {

        i++;

        nums[i] = nums[j];
    }
}

return nums.length == 0 ? 0 : i + 1;
```

This Two Pointers pattern is useful in many array problems involving duplicates, in-place modifications, and sorted data.